# Development

Use a configured package registry when available. A local alpha smoke test can instead use wheels
built from the OpenSDL framework checkout:

```bash
uv sync --find-links /path/to/OpenSDL/dist
./scripts/check.sh
./scripts/dev.sh
```

## Why `uv.lock` is ignored, and how to start committing it

A lockfile belongs in Git. This one does not yet. `uv sync --find-links` records the wheelhouse
directory as the registry for every OpenSDL distribution, and that path exists only on the machine
that ran the command, so a clone elsewhere cannot resolve the lock and is given no reason why.
`.gitignore` therefore excludes `uv.lock`, and this repository has no pinned resolution until you
reverse that:

1. point the laboratory at an index or artifact source every clone can reach;
2. delete the `uv.lock` line from `.gitignore`;
3. `rm -f uv.lock && uv lock` so the new resolution carries no wheelhouse path — confirm with
   `grep -c 'find-links\|registry = "\.\.' uv.lock`;
4. commit the lock, then set the repository variable `OPENSDL_PACKAGES_AVAILABLE=true` so CI runs
   the full job against it.

Pin the framework versions in `pyproject.toml` in the same change. The generated floors are capped
at the next minor, which stops an unrelated `uv sync` carrying this laboratory across a release
that changes a contract, but any release inside that range still satisfies them. Before a first
real run, replace each `>=` with `==` at the version you validated against.

## Database

The store's schema is owned by migrations that ship inside OpenSDL, so this repository needs no
migration files of its own.

```bash
uv run opensdl migrate --check   # report pending revisions, write nothing
uv run opensdl migrate           # bring the store to the current schema
```

Opening the laboratory for writing upgrades the store on the way in, so an ordinary `opensdl run`
after an OpenSDL upgrade also migrates it. Back the store up before upgrading OpenSDL: a
migration is not reversible, and `.opensdl/` holds every run this laboratory has recorded.

## Credentials

Write `${env:NAME}` in `opensdl.yaml` rather than a credential, and set the variable in `.env` or
the environment. A reference that does not resolve is an error naming the variable, so a missing
credential fails when the manifest loads instead of at an instrument. `.env` is ignored by Git;
`.env.example` records which variables this laboratory expects.

Add organization-specific behavior under `src/{{ package }}/`. Install public
or private adapters through normal Python dependencies and declare them in
`opensdl.yaml`. Every physical adapter should have a simulator and a deployment-
controlled hardware test plan.
